    "passage": "The conventions for wedding rings vary from country to country in Europe. In countries such as the UK, France, and Italy, it is common for people to wear rings on their left hands. Other countries like Russia, Germany, and Poland wear wedding rings on their right hands. In Netherlands, the difference in wedding ring placement is religious: Protestants wear the ring on the right, [[1]] Catholics wear it on their left. In some Orthodox regions, the ring is worn on the left hand, but it should [[2]] to the right hand during the wedding ceremony.\nThe wedding ring serves as a(n) [[3]] of the wearer’s marital status. Its circular shape symbolizes eternity, representing the wearer’s commitment to the spouse. People who prefer wedding rings on the left hand believe that it is closer to the heart and more convenient for right-handed individuals. [[4]], some people prefer wearing their wedding rings on the right hand, for it’s the hand they swear an oath with. Furthermore, it is the hand used for handshakes, enabling others to easily [[5]] their marital status at first sight. At any rate, the decision of on which hand to wear the wedding ring depends on cultural and personal beliefs.",
